Implement one PR:

M14.5A — Tasks + Configurable Action / Command Engine

Base this work on current main after merged M14.4.2 / PR #59.

This milestone builds the authoritative task/command model that the later
M14.5B RTS Map Interaction milestone will use.

This is primarily a DOMAIN / COMMAND-ENGINE milestone with a practical,
form-based Web UI.

DO NOT implement lasso, box selection, map multi-select, drag-to-command,
live GPS, navigation sessions or realtime yet.

==================================================
CORE PRODUCT MODEL
==================================================

Gasilko must support an RTS-like operational interaction model in the future:

SELECT RECIPIENT(S)
    ->
CHOOSE ACTION
    ->
CHOOSE TARGET
    ->
CONFIRM INTENT
    ->
SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE COMMAND
    ->
ACKNOWLEDGEMENT / EXECUTION / RESULT

However:

THE MAP IS NOT THE AUTHORITY.

Client selection does not grant permission.

COMMAND INTENT
!=
EXECUTION PLAN
!=
TELEMETRY

Example:

COMMAND INTENT:
"Unit A: go to Point X"

FUTURE NAVIGATION EXECUTION:
"current route, remaining distance, ETA"

FUTURE TELEMETRY:
"actual GPS position"

Never conflate these concepts.

==================================================
1. DO NOT HARDCODE FIRE SERVICE TACTICS
==================================================

Do NOT build the command engine around a fixed enum like:

EXTINGUISH
DEFEND
SEARCH
RESCUE
VENTILATE
WATER_SUPPLY
RECON

These are operational/tactical meanings that may differ by:

- organization,
- country,
- doctrine,
- incident type,
- language,
- future integrations.

Instead create versioned CONFIGURABLE ACTION DEFINITIONS.

The command engine understands only a very small set of native execution
behaviors.

Everything else is configuration.

==================================================
2. NATIVE BEHAVIOR KERNEL
==================================================

Implement only these native behaviors:

NONE

MOVE_TO

HOLD_POSITION

WITHDRAW_TO

REQUEST_STATUS

These are SYSTEM SEMANTICS, not organization-defined tactical doctrine.

Do not add more native behaviors without a concrete engine-level reason.

Specifically:

ACKNOWLEDGE is NOT an Action Definition.
It is command-assignment lifecycle.

CANCEL is NOT an Action Definition.
It is command/task lifecycle.

COMPLETE is NOT an Action Definition.
It is execution lifecycle.

ASSIGN_SECTOR is NOT an Action Definition.
It remains the existing authoritative M14.4 structural operation.

UNIT_LEADER assignment is NOT an Action Definition.

Crew membership is NOT an Action Definition.

Resource allocation is NOT an Action Definition.

RTS UX may expose those structural operations contextually later,
but they must continue using their owning authoritative APIs.

==================================================
3. NATIVE BEHAVIOR SEMANTICS
==================================================

NONE

No engine-specific execution behavior.
The Action Definition describes allowed recipients/targets/parameters.
Human lifecycle updates remain authoritative.

MOVE_TO

Represents movement INTENT only.

It does NOT:

- modify unit position,
- calculate a route,
- start navigation,
- create ETA,
- infer arrival,
- move a marker,
- write GPS data.

A MOVE_TO Action Definition must use a point-resolvable target.

Allowed target classes for native MOVE_TO should be conservative:

- COORDINATE
- HYDRANT
- point INCIDENT_MAP_OBJECT

Do NOT automatically use a polygon centroid.

Do NOT automatically use a sector centroid.

If the future UI wants "move into Sector A", it must ultimately provide an
explicit movement destination or another future execution rule.

HOLD_POSITION

Represents an instruction to remain/operate at the current declared context.

No fake coordinate is created.

Target may be NONE or a valid explicitly selected operational target as
per the Action Definition.

WITHDRAW_TO

Represents explicit withdrawal / retreat intent.

This is semantically different from ordinary MOVE_TO because later safety
UX/notification behavior may treat it differently.

Default priority should be CRITICAL or equivalent highest operational
priority.

It still does NOT move a marker or calculate a route.

REQUEST_STATUS

Requests an operational response/status.

No spatial target required.

Do not invent polling/realtime behavior yet.

