==================================================
13. ASSIGNMENT LIFECYCLE
==================================================

Use an explicit lifecycle.

Suggested:

ISSUED

ACKNOWLEDGED

IN_PROGRESS

BLOCKED

COMPLETED

UNABLE

CANCELLED

Rules:

ISSUED
-> ACKNOWLEDGED
-> IN_PROGRESS
-> COMPLETED

Where requires_acknowledgement = false:

ISSUED
-> IN_PROGRESS

Valid failure/exception paths:

ISSUED -> UNABLE
ACKNOWLEDGED -> UNABLE
IN_PROGRESS -> BLOCKED
BLOCKED -> IN_PROGRESS
BLOCKED -> UNABLE

Issuer-authorized cancellation:

nonterminal -> CANCELLED

Terminal:

COMPLETED
UNABLE
CANCELLED

No transition out of terminal state.

Require bounded reason for BLOCKED / UNABLE where applicable.

Do not delete assignment history.

==================================================
14. TASK AGGREGATE STATUS
==================================================

Parent task lifecycle must reflect child assignments without destroying
per-recipient evidence.

Recommended parent statuses:

OPEN

CLOSED

CANCELLED

Recommended final outcome for CLOSED:

SUCCESS

PARTIAL

FAILED

Server updates/recomputes aggregate state atomically when an assignment
enters a terminal state.

Suggested semantics:

all assignments COMPLETED
-> CLOSED / SUCCESS

some COMPLETED and some UNABLE/CANCELLED
-> CLOSED / PARTIAL

all terminal and none COMPLETED
-> CLOSED / FAILED

Explicit whole-task cancellation:
-> CANCELLED

Do not mark a command successful merely because one of several recipients
completed it.

==================================================
15. TASK CANCELLATION
==================================================

Issuer-authorized users may cancel:

- an individual nonterminal assignment, where scope permits;
- or the whole task.

Whole-task cancellation atomically:

- marks every remaining nonterminal assignment CANCELLED;
- preserves already terminal assignments;
- records actor/time;
- writes timeline/audit evidence;
- updates aggregate task state.

Cancellation is NOT deletion.

